{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Chalmers assumes the system can do Peano arithmetic and invokes that ability to construct G. Lean instead assumes diagonalization directly in T₀, for every one-variable formula. This supplies (5) when applied to ¬𝔅(x), but does not itself establish the fixed point from Peano arithmetic or require the system to do Peano arithmetic."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The passage uses one assertion relation. Lean splits it between T and T₀: in (1) a proof of A in T must yield a proof of 𝔅 A in T₀, and (2) and (3) must be provable in T₀. These are stronger requirements for a fixed weaker T₀, but taking T₀ = T recovers the single-system conditions."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean models unassailable assertion by classical first-order derivability and the belief symbol by any formula 𝔅 satisfying the derivability conditions. It does not express what makes an assertion unassailable or establish that 𝔅 describes actual belief. Thus ¬𝔅⊥ has the intended reading under the belief interpretation; it need not be a standard arithmetical consistency sentence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean makes syntactic infrastructure explicit: a universe-0 language with decidable symbol equality and numerals for its own sentences. The passage presupposes Gödel numbering but does not spell out these language restrictions. They do not impose computability on the system's assertions."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean presents (2) in curried form with the premises reordered, and states the resulting contradiction as T proving every sentence. These are equivalent to the passage's formulations in the classical logic being used."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "With T₀ = T and 𝔅 read as belief, the target captures the conditional: if the system asserts ¬𝔅⊥, then under (1)–(3) and the diagonal resources it is inconsistent. Neither the passage nor the target assumes that the system is computational."
}
